  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and thunderstorms today with outflow gusts\n   of 25-45 mph and heavy rain rates up to 0.5 inch per hour. \n\n-  Persistent active pattern late week into the weekend under\n   southwesterly flow.\n\nA well-defined upper-level disturbance over northern California \nearly this morning and its associated cold front will move across \nthe Northern Rockies today. Showers and thunderstorms will \ninitiate over north-central Idaho around sunrise, then track \nnortheastward across the Continental Divide by mid-afternoon. Main\nthreats include brief heavy rainfall and erratic outflow wind \ngusts of 25-45 mph (locally higher in northwest Montana and along \nthe Divide with a 5% probability of severe gusts). Winds will \nsubside behind this initial wave for most areas, though elevated \ngradient winds will persist through sunset across Lemhi County and\nsouthwest Montana. Scattered post-frontal showers will fill in \nthis afternoon and continue into Thursday. High confidence \n(60-80%) remains for widespread wetting rain (0.10 inches or \ngreater) through Thursday morning, with the exception of far \nsouthwest Montana.\n\nOne exception is across far southwest Montana, the combination of\ngusty winds and low relative humidity will drive critical fire \nweather conditions from this afternoon through Thursday afternoon,\nwith elevated conditions lingering into Friday as the cold front \nis slow to pass through.\n\nPersistent southwesterly flow aloft will remain Thursday into \nFriday as a broad upper-level trough settles over the Pacific \nNorthwest. This pattern will support near- to slightly below-\nnormal late-summer temperatures along with daily scattered showers\nand thunderstorms during peak afternoon and evening heating. \nGuidance is in solid agreement that troughing will linger through \nthe weekend. A more notable shortwave may eject across the region \non Monday, with some ensemble solutions supporting a closed low \ntapping into tropical moisture. NBM probabilities of precipitation\nexceeding 0.25 inches currently stand at 50-70% over a broad area\non Monday.",
